implementationPlan.note:
Durability + isolation landed against a REAL file-backed SqliteEventStore. Suite 34/34, daemon typecheck exit 0. Every store handle is closed in a `finally` before its temp directory is removed — a handle held across cleanup throws EPERM on Windows and kills the vitest worker with no output at all.

WHAT THE STEP ASKED FOR, AND WHERE IT IS:
- byte-stable committed-then-read round trip, with a real CLOSE and REOPEN between write and read (the reopen is the part that proves durability rather than in-handle memory);
- two different attempts -> disjoint aggregates and disjoint captureRefs, both written into ONE store and BOTH read back (reading only one would not have noticed a bleed);
- duplicate commit refuses and the ROW COUNT read back is asserted to be one, on both conflict arms;
- a record committed under one identity is unreachable through another's captureRef;
- determinism: the same preparation committed into TWO SEPARATE STORES yields byte-identical bytes, the same captureRef and the same aggregate. Running it twice in one store would only have proved the second call replayed.
- observedAt is a bound input and decidedAt is the store's decision instant: sealing the same preparation under a DIFFERENT decidedAt produces identical bytes and an identical ref. If decidedAt ever leaked into the preimage the same preparation would seal differently on every retry and the replay path would become unreachable.

A DEFECT IN MY OWN TEST, FOUND BY DRILLING AND FIXED — worth reading, because the test looked right.
The "no module-global map" test (task rail 2) originally read: commit into store A, then read a FRESH store B and require ABSENT. I drilled it by adding a real module-level Map cache to readFoundationCaptureContext. IT SURVIVED GREEN. The reason is that the commit path returns COMMITTED without reading, so it never populates a cache — the fresh-store read then finds the cache cold and answers ABSENT for the wrong reason. The test was vacuous against the exact defect it named. Fixed by making the order load-bearing: read the WRITTEN store first so any cache is WARM, and only then demand ABSENT from the fresh store. Re-drilled: 3 tests red.

The first version of that drill was ALSO wrong and I caught it the same way: it read from the cache but never wrote to it, so it was a mutation that applied nothing, which reads exactly like a passing test. The rerun carries a positive control printed alongside the verdict — cache-present=True bytes-changed=True — so "the drill applied" is evidence rather than an assumption.

Second drill this step: a counter injected into the identity frame, making the derivation impure -> 10 tests red, including both determinism tests. Ledger restored byte-exactly after every drill (sha256 3269fb702df769ea4d0f23423888b3f37cb75fd3ed72e1c05fba6adec65a60b4).

SCOPE HELD. No service launch order changed, no captureResult invoked, no catalog/worktree/scanner called. Identities and manifests arrive as fixture inputs shaped to the sibling contracts; the WorkspaceInputManifest and ScopeObservation fixtures come from the REAL producers buildInputManifest and observeScope (driven through observeScope's injected ports), so their seals are genuine rather than hand-typed. task-f146fa2e composes the real flow.
